# Proposal: reuse Carve layouts before adding syntax

Status: draft for discussion. This document changes no parser behavior,
configuration handling or styling.

## Recommendation

Keep column structure in ordinary Carve containers. Do not add `%% layout`,
`%% left` or `%% right` directives now. Their benefit is fewer container fences;
the cost is another structural syntax to parse, lint, export and explain.

The next authoring improvement should document and style the existing patterns.
Treat deck-level frontmatter as a separate convenience, with lower priority than
making shared layouts work consistently.

## Two existing column behaviors

Carve's [columns recipe](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/blob/main/docs/recipes.md)
uses ordinary containers and attributes:

```carve
{data-columns="2"}
::: columns
First run of text.

Second run of text.
:::
```

The [shared stylesheet](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve-css/blob/main/src/recipes.css)
uses CSS multicolumn flow. It keeps individual blocks together and collapses to
one column on narrow screens. It does not assign a particular paragraph to the
left or right column. The source is portable Carve; the visual layout requires
the recipe stylesheet and its `.carve` scope.

For a comparison with fixed sides, reveal-carve already has a grid. This is
supported source, with the class on the container opener:

```carve
## Adjust the balance

:::: two-col
::: before
### Before

20 minutes explaining.

5 minutes practicing.
:::

::: after
### After

10 minutes explaining.

15 minutes practicing.
:::
::::
```

Both forms use Carve's existing container grammar. The `two-col` styling belongs
to reveal-carve today; it should not be described as a shared ecosystem recipe.
Slidev's named layouts are more concise for fixed slots, but copying that model
would give Carve authors a second way to express an existing structure.

## Proposed next implementation

Document the difference between flowing columns and fixed comparison panels.
Use named container openers in examples where they remove an extra attribute
line without changing the structure.

Support the shared columns recipe in the reveal theme through a scoped styling
adapter or a reviewed import. Preserve its class name, `data-columns` attribute
and flow behavior. Check the `.carve` scope, theme variables and interactions
with reveal's sizing before choosing between those approaches. Keep the current
`two-col`, `before` and `after` classes working.

A fixed-panel recipe shared across Carve hosts could be useful later. Propose it
in carve-css if other hosts need it, using the existing container grammar.
Do not reinterpret the current flowing-columns recipe as a fixed grid.

Acceptance would cover both layouts in light and dark themes, runtime and built
slides, PDF and Markdown handouts. Verify reading order and that neither panel
is lost in export. Check narrow viewports against reveal's scaled slide canvas;
a CSS viewport breakpoint alone may not give the intended result. No core Carve
parser change should be needed.

## Is frontmatter worthwhile here?

Possibly, for deck configuration. Carve already preserves frontmatter in its
document AST, but leaves its contents uninterpreted. reveal-carve would still
need to define and validate a configuration schema.

An illustrative future schema could be:

```yaml
---
title: Planning a workshop
reveal:
  theme: white
  darkTheme: black
  renderers:
    - mermaid
    - katex
---
```

These keys are a proposal, not supported settings. They describe the deck rather
than where its paragraphs belong. Column structure would stay in the body.

The training starter already puts repeated options in `deck.mjs`. Frontmatter
would move those options closer to the source, but does not unlock a missing
capability. Defer implementation until that extra configuration location solves
a recurring authoring problem.

Before implementing it, settle one entry document for chapter-based decks,
precedence (explicit CLI/API options over metadata over defaults), supported
formats, unknown-key errors and a finite allowlist of settings. Exclude arbitrary
scripts, executable values and paths from the initial schema. Make build, watch,
source PDF and runtime behavior explicit for every supported setting.

Extract frontmatter before slide splitting: its `---` fences otherwise overlap
with the existing slide separator. Do not add per-slide frontmatter or treat
chapter and include metadata as implicit global overrides.

## Review decision

Proceed with existing layout examples and shared styling support first. Defer
new column directives and deck frontmatter. Revisit frontmatter when a concrete
workflow shows that maintaining `deck.mjs` is the source of repeated work.
